CPU AND REGISTER ORGANIZATION
The CPU of the PIC16F72 consists of the main processing unit along with registers used for instruction execution, data manipulation, memory addressing, and control.
The important registers and components related to CPU operation include:
•	Working (W) register
•	STATUS register
•	Program Counter (PC)
•	PCL and PCLATH registers
•	File Select Register (FSR)
•	Special Function Registers (SFRs)
•	General Purpose Registers (GPRs)
 Working Register (W)
The W register is an 8-bit working register used by the CPU for temporary storage of data during instruction execution. Many arithmetic, logical, and data-transfer instructions use the W register.
For example, the instruction:
MOVLW 25H
loads the value 25H into the W register.
The W register is therefore an important part of the instruction execution process and needs to be represented in the simulator.
 File Registers
The PIC16F72 uses file registers for storing and accessing data. They can be broadly divided into:
Special Function Registers (SFRs): Used to control and monitor different functions of the microcontroller.
General Purpose Registers (GPRs): Used by the program for storing temporary data.
Some important registers include the STATUS register, PCL, PCLATH, FSR, and I/O port registers.
 STATUS Register
The STATUS register contains information about the result of arithmetic and logical operations. It also contains bits used for register-bank selection and other processor status information.
The main status bits relevant to CPU operation are:
•	C – Carry flag
•	DC – Digit Carry flag
•	Z – Zero flag
•	TO – Time-out status bit
•	PD – Power-down status bit
•	RP0 and RP1 – Register bank selection bits




