Stack Organization
The PIC16F72 uses a hardware stack for storing return addresses during subroutine calls and interrupts.
Unlike processors that have a conventional software-accessible Stack Pointer (SP) register, the PIC16F72 uses a dedicated hardware stack. Therefore, the stack should not be treated as a normal data-memory stack with a directly accessible SP register.
The hardware stack is used mainly by instructions such as:
•	CALL
•	RETURN
When a CALL instruction is executed, the return address is stored in the stack. The processor then jumps to the subroutine.
When the RETURN instruction is executed, the stored return address is retrieved and loaded into the Program Counter.
