 Stack Implementation in the Simulator
The simulator should maintain an internal representation of the hardware stack. It can be implemented using an array or another suitable data structure.
The simulator should support:
Pushing a return address when a CALL occurs.
Popping the return address when a RETURN occurs.
Maintaining the correct stack level.
Handling stack operations associated with interrupts.
The internal stack representation is an implementation feature of the simulator and should not be confused with an actual user-accessible Stack Pointer register in the PIC16F72.


STATUS and Flag Information
The STATUS register provides information about the current state of the CPU and the results of arithmetic and logical operations.
The important bits are described below.

Carry Flag 
The Carry flag indicates whether an arithmetic operation produces a carry beyond the most significant bit of the 8-bit result.
For example:
11111111
+ 00000001
---------------
1 00000000

The 8-bit result is 00000000, while a carry is generated. Therefore, the Carry flag is set.
Digit Carry Flag (DC)
The Digit Carry flag indicates whether a carry occurs from bit 3 to bit 4 during an arithmetic operation.
This flag is useful for detecting carry between the lower and upper nibbles of an 8-bit value.
 Zero Flag (Z)
The Zero flag indicates whether the result of an operation is zero.
For example:
05H – 05H = 00H
Since the result is zero, the Zero flag is set.
If the result is non-zero, the Zero flag is cleared.
 Register Bank Selection Bits
The RP0 and RP1 bits are used for selecting the appropriate register bank in the data-memory organization.
The simulator must consider these bits when determining which file register is being accessed.


